.v2.schema.json, https://jaresty.github.io/lsp-trace/schemas/lsp-trace.unified-structural-context-result.v3.schema.json. Results are evidence bounded by the named schemas and do not establish source completeness, runtime execution, producer authentication, permission, or production authority. If this direct tool is hidden by the active advertisement profile, call lsp_trace_v1_execute with its canonical request instead. Before live semantic analysis, call lsp_session_v1_list and use an exact-workspace READY session. If none exists and the target is an exact registered Git worktree, call lsp_session_v1_derive_workspace with exactly one READY parent and the target's canonical absolute local file: URI; continue only from its READY result. Do not start an independently configured replacement language server. Choose the transport explicitly: Direct MCP operation calls pass that operation's arguments directly; for MCP gateway `lsp_trace_v1_execute`, operation is nested beneath `request`, for example {\"request\":{\"operation\":\"lsp_trace_v2_structural_context\",\"arguments\":{...}}}; CLI `lsp-trace execute` accepts production execution requests and is not an MCP dispatcher. MCP wire and server operation names are canonical unqualified names; host adapters may display namespace-qualified names. `request.operation` always uses the canonical unqualified operation name. If capabilities or the installed revision advertise an operation but the client does not list it or its tool schema rejects it before dispatch, reconnect or refresh MCP metadata before diagnosing the language server. The MCP gateway is a dispatch interface and does not bypass stale client-side schema validation. Distinguish client or adapter schema validation from server dispatch, structural preflight, and traversal typed failures.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__lsp_trace__lsp_trace_v2_structural_context(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"$id":"https://jaresty.github.io/lsp-trace/mcp/schemas/input-structural-context.v6.schema.json","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","additionalProperties":false,"oneOf":[{"not":{"anyOf":[{"required":["uri"]},{"required":["line"]},{"required":["character"]},{"required":["regex_locator"]}]},"required":["symbol"]},{"not":{"anyOf":[{"required":["symbol"]},{"required":["regex_locator"]}]},"required":["uri","line","character"]},{"not":{"anyOf":[{"required":["symbol"]},{"required":["uri"]},{"required":["line"]},{"required":["character"]}]},"required":["regex_locator"]},{"not":{"anyOf":[{"required":["line"]},{"required":["character"]},{"required":["regex_locator"]}]},"properties":{"down_depth":{"const":0},"projection":{"properties":{"include_relation_occurrences":{"const":false},"mode":{"const":"TARGET"}},"required":["mode","include_relation_occurrences"],"type":"object"},"up_depth":{"const":0}},"required":["symbol","uri","projection","up_depth","down_depth"]}],"properties":{"analysis":{"default":{"kind":"NEIGHBORHOOD"},"oneOf":[{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"kind":{"const":"NEIGHBORHOOD"}},"required":["kind"],"type":"object"},{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"depth":{"maximum":64,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"direction":{"enum":["INCOMING","OUTGOING"]},"kind":{"const":"IMPACT"}},"required":["kind","direction","depth"],"type":"object"}]},"character":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"down_depth":{"default":1,"maximum":64,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"generation":{"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"language_id":{"minLength":1,"type":"string"},"line":{"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_bytes":{"default":4194304,"maximum":16777216,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_messages":{"default":64,"maximum":4096,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_nodes":{"default":100,"maximum":10000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"projection":{"$id":"https://jaresty.github.io/lsp-trace/mcp/schemas/source-projection-request.v3.schema.json","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","additionalProperties":false,"description":"Optional bounded source projection. Omit this object for relationship-only structural context. For source-bearing implementation or ownership analysis, use PROJECTED with INCLUDE and FULL_DEFINITION.","properties":{"body":{"description":"INCLUDE returns source bodies for selected full display ranges; OMIT returns metadata, ranges, custody, privacy, and accounting without source bodies.","enum":["OMIT","INCLUDE"]},"display_range_policy":{"const":"FULL_DEFINITION","description":"Resolve complete definition display ranges from server-reported textDocument/documentSymbol while preserving exact evidence, item, selection, and call-site ranges independently."},"include_ancillary":{"description":"Include eligible ancillary source only when explicitly requested; normally false for implementation analysis.","type":"boolean"},"include_relation_occurrences":{"description":"Include exact server-reported call-site occurrences and their containing caller definitions when selected by the projection mode.","type":"boolean"},"limits":{"additionalProperties":false,"description":"Independent hard projection and document-acquisition bounds. A practical bounded starting point is objects/ranges 80, source bytes 2097152, work 10000, response bytes 8388608, additional documents 20, document requests 21, document bytes 1048576, total document bytes 8388608, document messages 32, acquisition work 21, and display-resolution work 21.","properties":{"max_additional_documents":{"description":"Maximum selected non-target documents.","maximum":10000,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_display_resolution_work":{"description":"Maximum server-reported document-symbol display-resolution work units.","maximum":536870912,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_document_acquisition_work":{"description":"Maximum managed document-acquisition work units.","maximum":536870912,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_document_bytes":{"description":"Maximum bytes admitted for each prepared document.","maximum":16777216,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_document_messages":{"description":"Maximum protocol messages consumed by each document preparation.","maximum":4096,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_document_requests":{"description":"Maximum managed document preparation requests including the target.","maximum":10000,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_objects":{"description":"Maximum selected projection units.","maximum":10000,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_ranges":{"description":"Maximum selected source ranges.","maximum":10000,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_response_bytes":{"description":"Maximum complete encoded response bytes.","maximum":16777216,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_source_bytes":{"description":"Maximum unique returned source bytes.","maximum":16777216,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_total_document_bytes":{"description":"Maximum total bytes admitted across prepared documents.","maximum":67108864,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"max_work":{"description":"Maximum projection work units.","maximum":536870912,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"}},"required":["max_objects","max_ranges","max_source_bytes","max_work","max_response_bytes","max_additional_documents","max_document_requests","max_document_bytes","max_total_document_bytes","max_document_messages","max_document_acquisition_work","max_display_resolution_work"],"type":"object"},"mode":{"description":"TARGET returns only the target definition; PROJECTED returns selected endpoint definitions and exact server-reported relation occurrences; COMPLETE_CAPTURE additionally permits explicitly selected ancillary source.","enum":["TARGET","PROJECTED","COMPLETE_CAPTURE"]},"paging":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"cursor":{"maxLength":16384,"minLength":1,"type":"string"},"max_page_bytes":{"maximum":1048576,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_pages":{"maximum":10000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_response_bytes":{"maximum":67108864,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"}},"required":["max_page_bytes","max_pages","max_response_bytes"],"type":"object"},"privacy_policy_id":{"description":"Caller-selected projection privacy policy identifier; use only a policy recognized by the executing host.","maxLength":256,"minLength":1,"type":"string"}},"required":["mode","body","include_relation_occurrences","include_ancillary","display_range_policy","limits","privacy_policy_id"],"type":"object"},"regex_locator":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"capture_group":{"maximum":32,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"expected_document_digest":{"pattern":"^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}$","type":"string"},"limits":{"additionalProperties":false,"properties":{"max_document_bytes":{"default":61440,"maximum":16777216,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_matches":{"default":100,"maximum":1000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_pattern_bytes":{"default":4096,"maximum":4096,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"max_work":{"default":65536,"maximum":536870912,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"}},"type":"object"},"match_index":{"maximum":999,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"pattern":{"maxLength":4096,"minLength":1,"type":"string"},"uri":{"format":"uri","type":"string"}},"required":["uri","pattern","match_index","limits"],"type":"object"},"request_timeout_ms":{"default":15000,"maximum":60000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"session_id":{"minLength":1,"type":"string"},"symbol":{"minLength":1,"type":"string"},"timeout_ms":{"default":30000,"maximum":60000,"minimum":1,"type":"integer"},"up_depth":{"default":1,"maximum":64,"minimum":0,"type":"integer"},"uri":{"format":"uri","type":"string"}},"required":["session_id","generation"],"type":"object"}},{"name":"mcp__granola__query_granola_meetings","description":"Query Granola about the user's meetings using natural language. Returns a tailored response with inline citation links in markdown (e.g. [[1]](url)) that reference source meeting notes.\n\nIMPORTANT: The response includes numbered citation links to specific Granola meeting notes. These citations MUST be preserved in your response to the user — they provide transparency and allow the user to verify information by clicking through to the original notes.\n\nFor optional deeper reads, extract the meeting ID from the citation URL: https://notes.granola.ai/d/<meeting_id>. Pass that ID to get_meetings (meeting_ids).\n\nThis tool is slow: an AI agent behind it searches and reads the user's notes before answering. Prefer a faster tool when it can answer the question:\n- list_meetings: find meetings by date range\n- get_meetings: read notes for meetings whose IDs you already have\n\nWhen to use:\n- User asks about what was discussed, decided, or action-items from meetings\n- User asks about follow-ups, todos, or commitments from recent meetings\n- User references 'Granola notes' or 'meeting notes'\n\nWhen NOT to use:\n- User is asking about calendar scheduling or upcoming events\n- User explicitly asks for a specific meeting by ID (use get_meetings instead)\n\nPrioritize using query_granola_meetings over list_meetings/get_meetings for open-ended or natural language queries about meeting content.\n\nIf this tool does not find a requested detail, meeting summaries may have omitted it. Use list_meetings/get_meetings to identify the most relevant meetings, then get_meeting_transcript only where that meeting's workspace permits transcript access. If access is denied, report that limitation; do not retry through another tool or conclude the information was never discussed.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__query_granola_meetings(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{"query":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"description":"The query to run on Granola meeting notes"},"document_ids":{"description":"Optional meeting UUIDs or public note IDs to limit context to (max 10).","maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}],"description":"Meeting UUID or public note ID (not_…)"}},"note_ids":{"description":"Alias for document_ids (max 10). If both are provided, they must refer to the same set of notes; order, duplicates, and UUID/public ID format may differ.","maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}],"description":"Meeting UUID or public note ID (not_…)"}}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"mcp__granola__list_meetings","description":"List the user's Granola meeting notes within a time range (the last 7 days by default). Returns meeting titles, metadata, and note URLs.\n\nIMPORTANT: For short-term questions about recent meeting details, prefer using query_granola_meetings instead.\n\nWhen to use:\n- User asks to list their meetings\n- User asks about action items, decisions, or summaries from meetings over a longer or specific date range\n- User asks about content from their meeting transcripts\n- User references 'Granola notes' or 'meeting notes' or 'transcripts'\n\nWhen NOT to use:\n- User is asking about upcoming calendar events or scheduling\n- User wants to create/modify calendar invites\n\nFiltering:\n- Omit workspace_only and involvement to return every meeting the user is allowed to access\n- For 'my meetings' or meetings the user was involved in, set both involvement conditions to true; true conditions combine using OR\n- Set captured_by_me to true for meetings whose Granola notes the user captured, or false to exclude them\n- Set listed_as_participant to true for meetings where the user is a known participant, or false to exclude them\n- False involvement conditions are exclusions and combine with the positive conditions using AND; omit a condition to ignore it\n- For Team Space or public workspace meetings, set workspace_only to true\n- workspace_only and involvement combine using AND\n\nUse get_meetings to retrieve detailed meeting content after identifying relevant meetings.\nUse list_meeting_folders to discover folder IDs, then pass a folder_id to list meetings within a specific folder.\nThe requested_range element reports the range that was queried, in the user's timezone. The from and to attributes on meetings_data are the oldest and newest meetings returned.\nMeetings with shared-screen screenshots you can view include a screenshot_count attribute; a meeting without it has none you can view. Use get_meetings to read what each screenshot shows.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__list_meetings(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{"time_range":{"description":"Time range to query meetings from. Custom bounds imply 'custom' when time_range is omitted; otherwise defaults to the last 7 days, starting at midnight in the user's timezone.","type":"string","enum":["last_7_days","this_week","last_week","last_30_days","custom"]},"custom_start":{"description":"ISO date for custom range start (required if time_range is 'custom'). Prefer YYYY-MM-DD for whole-day requests; dates and times without an offset use the user's stored timezone (UTC if unavailable). An explicit Z means UTC; explicit offsets are respected. Do not append Z to a local time.","type":"string"},"custom_end":{"description":"ISO date for custom range end (required if time_range is 'custom'). Prefer YYYY-MM-DD to include the whole final day in the user's stored timezone (UTC if unavailable). Times without an offset use that timezone; an explicit Z means UTC and explicit offsets are respected. Do not append Z to a local time.","type":"string"},"folder_id":{"description":"Optional folder ID to list meetings from a specific folder. Requires a paid Granola plan. Use list_meeting_folders to discover folder IDs.","type":"string"},"workspace_only":{"description":"Set to true only when the user explicitly asks for Team Space, public workspace, or workspace-visible meetings. Omit to search every meeting the user is allowed to access.","type":"boolean","const":true},"involvement":{"description":"Optional involvement filter. Conditions set to true form an OR inclusion group. Conditions set to false are exclusions applied using AND. Omitted conditions are ignored.","type":"object","properties":{"captured_by_me":{"description":"True includes meetings whose Granola note was captured by the connected user; false excludes them; omit to ignore who captured the note. This identifies the note owner, not necessarily the meeting organizer.","type":"boolean"},"listed_as_participant":{"description":"True includes meetings where the connected user's email appears in Granola's known participant metadata; false excludes them; omit to ignore participant metadata. Participant metadata can be incomplete and does not prove attendance.","type":"boolean"}},"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"mcp__granola__list_meeting_folders","description":"List the user's Granola meeting folders. Returns folder ID, title, description, note count including nested folders, visibility (private or workspace) and who can see the folder's notes.\n\nWhen to use:\n- User asks about their folders or wants to browse meetings by folder\n- User wants to narrow down meeting searches to a specific folder\n\nUse the returned folder IDs with list_meetings (folder_id parameter) to list meetings within a specific folder.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__list_meeting_folders(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{}}},{"name":"mcp__granola__get_meetings","description":"Get detailed meeting information for one or more Granola meetings by ID. Returns private notes, AI-generated summary, attendees, metadata, and note URLs.\nDoes not return transcripts. Notes and summaries can omit details that were discussed.\nIf a detail is missing, use get_meeting_transcript on the most relevant meetings only where that meeting's workspace permits transcript access. If access is denied, report that limitation; do not retry through another tool or conclude it was never discussed.\nUse this when you already have specific meeting IDs (e.g. from list_meetings results). For open-ended questions about meeting content, use query_granola_meetings instead.\nMeetings where the user captured the shared screen include an <attachments> section listing each screenshot as an <attachment type=\"screenshot\"> with an ID and description. Descriptions are lossy. For questions about designs, UI, slides, charts, code or anything else visual, view the relevant screenshots with get_meeting_attachment rather than answering from descriptions.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__get_meetings(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{"meeting_ids":{"description":"Meeting UUIDs or public note IDs (max 10).","minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}],"description":"Meeting UUID or public note ID (not_…)"}},"note_ids":{"description":"Alias for meeting_ids. If both are provided, they must refer to the same set of notes; order, duplicates, and UUID/public ID format may differ.","minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}],"description":"Meeting UUID or public note ID (not_…)"}}},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"mcp__granola__get_meeting_transcript","description":"Get the full transcript for a specific Granola meeting by ID. Returns only the verbatim transcript content, not summaries or notes.\nTranscript blocks separate audio source (Microphone/System audio) from speaker identity. Participant attribution and user-confirmed names are preserved. Desktop diarization is omitted; mobile/imported transcripts can retain Speaker A/B/... labels for unnamed voices. Recording context identifies the document owner as the recorder for native recordings, not as the speaker. Missing recorder data stays unknown; the current viewer is not substituted. Channels and room labels may include multiple people. Older preformatted transcripts keep their original labels.\nRequires access to the meeting and transcript access permitted by that meeting's workspace. Use this for exact quotes or when query_granola_meetings/get_meetings does not surface a requested detail, such as a person, example, or explanation. Start with the most relevant meetings; a detail missing from summaries may still be in the transcript. If access is denied, report that limitation and do not retry through another tool. For general summaries or action items, start with query_granola_meetings or get_meetings.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__get_meeting_transcript(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{"meeting_id":{"description":"Meeting UUID or public note ID (not_…).","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}]},"note_id":{"description":"Alias for meeting_id. If both are provided, they must refer to the same note (UUID and public note ID may differ).","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}]}},"additionalProperties":false}},{"name":"mcp__granola__get_account_info","description":"Get the email, active workspace, joined workspaces (names and IDs), and effective note-access scopes for the Granola account currently connected to this MCP session.\n\nWorkspace membership does not imply MCP access to its meetings.\n\nmcp_plan is the effective plan used for MCP access and limits.\n\nWhen to use:\n- User asks 'who am I signed in as?', 'which Granola account is this?', or 'what's my email?'\n- User suspects they connected the wrong Granola account\n- User wants to sign out or switch accounts: use sign_out to revoke this client's authorization. No browser action is needed to complete MCP sign-out. To use another account, reconnect Granola in this client. Only if the browser automatically selects the previous account, open sign_out_url in that browser before reconnecting again.\n- User asks which workspaces they belong to or mentions another workspace\n- Meeting results may be missing because the wrong workspace is active\n- Meeting results may be incomplete or empty because of the user's Granola plan or workspace MCP access controls\n- Before performing actions, to confirm identity if the user references a specific account\n\nmcp_note_access.scopes contains the note categories this connection can search:\n- personal: notes the connected user owns, notes shared directly with them, and notes accessible through private folders\n- public: notes accessible through workspace-visible Team Space folders\n- Both scopes means both categories can be searched. These scopes describe access, not who captured or participated in a meeting.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__get_account_info(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{}}},{"name":"mcp__granola__sign_out","description":"Revoke the connected Granola account's consent and refresh tokens for this MCP client. Only use when the user asks to sign out, disconnect, or switch Granola accounts. This affects all connections for this account using the same client authorization. New MCP requests using this authorization will be rejected immediately after sign-out. No browser action is needed to complete MCP sign-out. If the user wants to switch accounts, tell them to reconnect Granola in this client. Only if the browser automatically selects their previous account, open the returned sign_out_url in that browser before reconnecting again.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__sign_out(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{}}},{"name":"mcp__granola__get_meeting_attachment","description":"Get up to 5 meeting attachments, such as screenshots of shared screens, as images in request order. Provide attachment_ids for a batch or attachment_id for one image. Each image is followed by its metadata; failed attachments return an error with their ID without discarding successful images. Attachment IDs come from the <attachments> section of get_meetings results. Use this when the description alone does not answer the user's question, for example to read a slide, chart, or code on screen.\n\nCodemode: `tools.mcp__granola__get_meeting_attachment(args)` resolves to `CallToolResult`.","parameters":{"type":"object","$schema":"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema","properties":{"meeting_id":{"description":"Meeting UUID or public note ID (not_…).","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}]},"note_id":{"description":"Alias for meeting_id. If both are provided, they must refer to the same note (UUID and public note ID may differ).","anyOf":[{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12})$"},{"type":"string","pattern":"^not_[a-zA-Z0-9]{14}$"}]},"attachment_id":{"description":"Single attachment ID. Provide exactly one of attachment_id or attachment_ids.","type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[1-8][0-9a-fA-F]{3}-[89abAB][0-9a-fA-F]{3}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}|00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000|ffffffff-ffff-ffff-ffff-ffffffffffff)$"},"attachment_ids":{"description":"Attachment IDs from the <attachment id=\"...\"> tags in get_meetings results. Provide exactly one of attachment_id or attachment_ids.","minItems":1,"maxItems":5,"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","format":"uuid","pattern":"^([0-9a-fA-F]{8}-[0-9a-fA-F]{4}-[1-8][0-9a-fA-F]{3}-[89abAB][0-9a-fA-F]{3}-[0-9a-fA-F]{12}|00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000|ffffffff-ffff-ffff-ffff-ffffffffffff)$"}}},"additionalProperties":false}}]},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:40:08.863Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"error","timestamp":1791906008726,"diagnostics":[{"type":"provider_transport_failure","timestamp":1791906008764,"error":{"name":"Error","message":"WebSocket error","stack":"Error: WebSocket error\n    at extractWebSocketError (file:///opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/bundle/chunks/openai-codex-responses-4MYQSO5E.js:10:8322)\n    at WebSocket.onError (file:///opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/bundle/chunks/openai-codex-responses-4MYQSO5E.js:10:10245)\n    at [nodejs.internal.kHybridDispatch] (node:internal/event_target:843:20)\n    at WebSocket.dispatchEvent (node:internal/event_target:776:26)\n    at fireEvent (file:///opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/bundle/chunks/chunk-H33F2TZD.js:129:104362)\n    at #onSocketClose (file:///opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/bundle/chunks/chunk-H33F2TZD.js:129:135096)\n    at TLSSocket.onSocketClose (file:///opt/homebrew/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/bundle/chunks/chunk-H33F2TZD.js:129:128165)\n    at TLSSocket.emit (node:events:521:24)\n    at node:net:355:12\n    at TCP.done (node:internal/tls/wrap:769:7)"},"details":{"configuredTransport":"auto","fallbackTransport":"sse","eventsEmitted":false,"phase":"before_message_stream_start","requestBytes":179112}}],"errorMessage":"fetch failed","thinkingLevel":"minimal"},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:40:08.864Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"toolResult","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_YvCSLPTsa1vIS6Vlzg8Sn2HL|fc_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e2a30481919026e4752bf23233","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1\t---\n2\tname: lsp-trace-feature-inventory\n3\tdescription: Prepare and review correction-safe feature inventories from bounded lsp-trace structural evidence. Use for mechanical inventory preparation, reversible grouping hypotheses, semantic adjudication, stakeholder correction, and accepted-inventory boundaries; never use structural evidence alone to accept feature identity.\n4\t---\n5\t\n6\t# lsp-trace feature inventory\n7\t\n8\tUse this workflow when bounded technical evidence must inform a feature inventory. The inventory is external to native lsp-trace graph, inspection, filter, community, instability, and capture-set artifacts.\n9\t\n10\tLoad references progressively:\n11\t\n12\t- For evidence packet preparation, census inputs, accounting, provisional records, and reversible grouping, read [Preparation and grouping](references/preparation-and-grouping.md).\n13\t- For independent semantic review, stakeholder correction, acceptance, presentation, iteration, and stopping rules, read [Adjudication and acceptance](references/adjudication-and-acceptance.md).\n14\t- For structural command details or authority ceilings, also load the `lsp-trace` skill and its relevant reference.\n15\t\n16\t## Non-negotiable boundary\n17\t\n18\tMechanical preparation cannot accept feature identity. Technical adjacency, CALLS overlap, communities, centrality, instability, repeated labels, capture-set co-membership, or matching names are structural evidence only. They may nominate or challenge a hypothesis; they cannot decide canonical identity, purpose, value, production use, merge/split outcome, coverage, confidence, or acceptance.\n19\t\n20\tCALLS remains server-reported only. Capture sets preserve constituents without inferring cross-capture CALLS or acquiring native single-capture custody. Preserve bounded zero, `UNKNOWN`, failed/successful-empty states, and untouched ambiguity explicitly.\n21\t\n22\t## Workflow\n23\t\n24\t1. Retain a transport-neutral technical evidence packet and reconcile all seeds and counts.\n25\t2. Prepare external `PROVISIONAL` candidate records with exact typed evidence references, alternatives, rejection conditions, authority ceilings, and correction lineage.\n26\t3. Treat grouping as a reversible hypothesis. Communities and instability may organize review but do not change candidate identity or status.\n27\t4. Run independent semantic adjudication. Challenge unsupported merges/splits, borrowed union evidence, transitive-as-direct claims, and absence inferred from bounded zero or `UNKNOWN`.\n28\t5. Request stakeholder correction for product/domain meaning. Record corrections as revisions with predecessors and finite inventory-state deltas; never rewrite native evidence.\n29\t6. Accept an inventory only under an explicit external authority and acceptance contract. Otherwise stop as provisional, unresolved, externally adjudicated, accepted-boundary, or bounded-review-dry as applicable.\n30\t\n31\tThe documented CLI `census` command may be used for accountable source-symbol enumeration and batched acquisition; use only syntax printed by current executable help. Its structural output cannot accept feature identity or upgrade authority, completeness, custody, cross-capture CALLS, or Leiden admission. `context` is AVAILABLE for bounded transient live structural analysis; use only current help or capability syntax and do not treat its output as retained evidence, feature identity, architecture, safety, or authority.\n\n## Related notes\n- 20260830220254-9113 — A provisional feature inventory is an evidence-bounded stakeholder adjudication surface [possibly relevant]\n- 20260824201733-0963 — Bounded provisional feature inventory v0.1 classifies 15 cross-product challenge cases [possibly relevant]\n- 20260824205433-1035 — Coverage-bounded provisional feature inventory v0.2 accounts for 33 three-product candidates [possibly relevant]\n- 20260824191243-1140 — A versioned provisional feature identity rule classifies three products while preserving correction lineage [possibly relevant]\n- 20260825163852-0028 — The 33-candidate provisional inventory is ready for a bounded evidence-tier view [possibly relevant]\nResolve each unread related note — run `nn show <id>` to open, or write `skip-related: <id> [<id> ...] — <reason>` to dismiss. Notes marked [read] have already been loaded this session and do not require action.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791906019433},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:40:19.434Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"toolResult","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_jXPzIQnUlZNroKxpCnbmVZKI|fc_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e2a320819182792d7db779b96a","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nid: 20260707222228-6219\ntitle: 'Protocol: route requirement-governed work by derivation step'\ntype: protocol\nstatus: permanent\ntags: []\napplies_when: before any substantive Bar build for work governed by stated requirements, observed evidence, or a mixture of both; and before the first file mutation in each Bar-built phase\ncreated: 2026-07-07T22:22:28.188177Z\nmodified: 2026-10-12T21:35:50.170982-07:00\n---\nfreshness: fresh (11 hours ago — likely current)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## Protocol\n\nRoute each substantive Bar build by the immediate step's derivation direction. Artifact type, file editing, technical subject matter, and exploratory tone do not select a route by themselves.\n\nA **governed build** is one substantive `bar build` command. Every governed build must be owned by exactly one final routing envelope. The final envelope is the last structured block before the tool call: after `Route complete.`, invoke the exact `command` immediately, with no intervening prose or tool call.\n\n## Ordered routing predicates\n\nApply these predicates in order to the immediate step and stop at the first match:\n\n1. **Inductive:** the step generalizes an understanding, dependency, or rule from bounded interventions, observed resistance, measurements, repository inspection, or other current-world evidence. Select `inductive` and use `bar build probe depends atomic --subject \"<question, bounded intervention, and observation target>\"`. This build is the evidence-workflow handoff; there is no separate abstract transition and no second receipt for the handoff. Subsequent evidence operations are governed by the complementary inductive-work protocol.\n2. **Craft:** otherwise, the step constructs or modifies executable behavior, a guard, or another artifact whose governing property requires a counterfactual witness. Select `craft` and use `bar build craft --subject \"<work and governing properties>\"`.\n3. **Audit:** otherwise, the step derives, revises, reviews, or evaluates a specification, plan, analysis, documentation artifact, or other non-executable outcome from stated requirements. Select `audit` and use `bar build make witness full ground presume atomic --subject \"<artifact or requirements>\" --addendum \"<requested analysis or revision>\"`. In this composition, `ground` first binds claims to requirements and evidence; `presume` acts only on reversible choices that remain open after grounding. Every such choice must carry its required presumption and revision trigger. `presume` never substitutes for evidence and never authorizes irreversible, production, externally consequential, or authority-bearing choices; those remain deferred.\n\nIf no predicate matches, this protocol authorizes no build. Requirements existing, a file being edited, a technical deliverable, or exploratory tone are not sufficient predicates.\n\n## Mixed tasks\n\nA task is mixed when its immediate steps match different predicates. Before its first governed build, divide it into explicitly named phases. Each phase has one immediate step, one independently selected route, and one final envelope. Re-evaluate from predicate 1 whenever derivation direction changes; never carry a route across phases.\n\nA deductive criteria phase uses `audit`. A current-world evidence phase uses `inductive`. A later repair is a new phase and is routed from its own immediate step.\n\n## Conflict correction before the final envelope\n\nBefore emitting the final envelope, compare the immediate step against **every other routing predicate**, including predicates earlier and later than the selected one.\n\nIf another predicate also matches, emit:\n\n```text\nRoute correction:\n  phase: <phase name>\n  conflict: found\n  selected: <candidate route>\n  competing: <other matching route>\n  reason: <why both match>\nRoute correction complete.\n```\n\nThen revise the phase boundary or selection and repeat the all-other-predicate comparison. A `Route correction:` block never authorizes a build. Continue until exactly one predicate matches. The final envelope must then contain `conflict: not_found` and list every other predicate in `conflict_checked`.\n\n## Final routing envelope\n\nEmit exactly this structure, filling every field:\n\n```text\nRoute:\n  phase: <single phase name>\n  action: <single immediate action>\n  derivation: stated-properties | observed-evidence\n  artifact: executable | guard | specification | analysis | other\n  selected: craft | audit | inductive\n  basis: 1 | 2 | 3\n  command_source: protocol-default | user-directed\n  requested_intent: <concise statement of the user preference> | none\n  resolved_tokens: <canonical Bar token string>\n  orchestration: none | in-thread | delegate | parallel | dialogue\n  normalization: <material syntax or token normalization disclosed> | none\n  conflict_checked: <comma-separated names of every other predicate>\n  conflict: not_found\n  gate:\n    status: admitted | omitted\n    prior_result: <verbatim qualifying result identity> | none\n    condition: <condition satisfied by that result> | none\n    blocked_action: <later action blocked by the condition> | none\n  falsify:\n    status: admitted | omitted\n    property: <required property> | none\n    guard: <observation-producing procedure> | none\n    violating_state: <admissible state the guard distinguishes> | none\n  command: <exact bar build command to invoke next>\nRoute complete.\n```\n\nThe `phase`, `action`, `derivation`, `artifact`, `selected`, `basis`, `command_source`, `requested_intent`, `resolved_tokens`, `orchestration`, `normalization`, `conflict_checked`, `conflict`, both admission substructures, and `command` fields are mandatory. `Route:` and `Route complete.` define the envelope boundary. Exactly one final envelope immediately precedes and owns exactly one governed build.\n\nThe `basis` value must identify the selected predicate, `derivation` must be `observed-evidence` only for predicate 1, `conflict_checked` must name the other two predicates, and `conflict` must be `not_found`; otherwise do not build.\n\nEach routing predicate defines its **protocol-default** command. Set `command_source: protocol-default` when the user has not expressed a clear preference for a different Bar workflow or token combination. In that case `requested_intent` and `normalization` must be `none`, `resolved_tokens` must contain the selected predicate's default tokens, and `command` must use that default build.\n\nSet `command_source: user-directed` when the user expresses a clear preference for a Bar workflow, token combination, reasoning style, or orchestration shape. Prefer the user's clear intent over the protocol default. The preference may be stated in natural language and need not contain an exact command or canonical token names. Resolve it cooperatively using the current Bar grammar and token or skill contracts. This resolution changes only the build method: `selected`, `basis`, and `derivation` remain determined by the ordered routing predicates.\n\nRecord the user's meaning concisely in `requested_intent` and the canonical token sequence in `resolved_tokens`. The assistant may normalize aliases, token order, spelling, and required command arguments. Record only material normalization in `normalization`; routine insertion of `--subject` or `--addendum` is not material. The `command` field must use `resolved_tokens` and may add arguments needed to bind the build to the immediate action.\n\nSet `orchestration` from the user's clear preference when supplied; otherwise derive it only when the applicable current Bar contract requires a particular shape, and otherwise use `none`. Token- and workflow-specific orchestration rules belong to their owning Bar contracts, not this protocol.\n\nDo not resist, downgrade, or silently replace a clear user preference merely because it is informal or requires syntax normalization. Ask for clarification only when the preference is materially ambiguous, internally contradictory, impossible under current Bar grammar, or incompatible with the immediate action. If no clear preference can be resolved, use the protocol default.\n\n## Optional token admission\n\n`gate.status: admitted` is valid only when `prior_result`, `condition`, and `blocked_action` are all non-`none`; the named result must be a qualifying prior command, test, or endpoint result at an earlier transcript position, and the blocked action must be later than that result. If any field is unavailable, set status to `omitted` and all three fields to `none`. When admitted, add `gate` to the envelope's command and obey its current token contract.\n\n`falsify.status: admitted` is valid only when `property`, `guard`, and `violating_state` are all non-`none`; the guard must be applicable and capable of distinguishing that violating state from a satisfying state. If any field is unavailable, set status to `omitted` and all three fields to `none`. Evidence-backed observation alone does not admit `falsify`. When admitted, add `falsify` to the envelope's command and obey its current token contract.\n\n## Bar skill-order check\n\nBefore the session's first `bar skills get` or `bar build`, inspect the earlier transcript for a successful `bar skills list` result and emit exactly one of:\n\n```text\nSkill order: pass\nSkill order: violated\n```\n\nEmit `Skill order: pass` only when the successful list result appears earlier than the first relevant Bar action. If the order was violated, it cannot be repaired retroactively: emit `Skill order: violated`, run `bar skills list`, load the required skill, and disclose that the first action remains noncompliant. The refreshed state permits only subsequent builds.\n\nIf Bar grammar, token semantics, or named workflows may have changed, consult the current Bar skill and CLI before selecting or executing a command.\n\n\n## Phase boundaries and route lifetime\n\nEvery governed build belongs to exactly one explicitly delimited phase. Before the final `Route:` envelope, emit exactly one phase-opening block:\n\n```text\nPhase:\n  id: <unique label within this response>\n  action: <one immediate action>\n  derivation: stated-properties | observed-evidence\n  artifact: executable | guard | specification | analysis | other\nPhase begin.\n```\n\nThe `phase`, `action`, `derivation`, and `artifact` values in the immediately following `Route:` envelope must exactly equal the phase-opening values. The `Route:` envelope must still immediately precede its one `bar build` tool call. A phase opening owns only that build and the execution of the prompt produced by that build; it does not authorize work matching another routing predicate.\n\nA governed build subject must describe actions matching exactly one ordered routing predicate. A subject that combines evidence-derived investigation with later executable repair, or otherwise combines actions matching different predicates, is invalid even when the later action is conditional on evidence. Split it into separate phases and separate builds before invoking Bar.\n\nAfter completing or blocking the phase, emit exactly one closing block:\n\n```text\nPhase result:\n  id: <matching phase id>\n  status: completed | blocked\n  result: <verbatim qualifying result identity>\nPhase end.\n```\n\nThe phase route expires at `Phase end.` A later governed action requires a new `Phase begin.`, final `Route:` envelope, and Bar build. No earlier route, Bar output, conditional instruction, RED result, gate receipt, or atomic-step label authorizes a later phase.\n\nBefore the first governed action after any `Phase end.` or after any change to the action, derivation, or artifact fields, run this corrective protocol:\n\n```text\nRoute-leak check:\n  quoted rule: \"A phase route expires at Phase end.\"\n  candidate action: <next governed action>\n  prior phase: <id>\n  current phase: <id or none>\nRoute leak: found | not_found\n```\n\n`Route leak: found` requires stopping before the action, emitting a new phase opening and route, and repeating the check. `Route leak: not_found` is terminal only when the candidate action is attached to a current phase whose opening values exactly match its route envelope.\n\nA transcript is structurally noncompliant when a `bar build` lacks an immediately owning `Route:` envelope; when a route lacks a preceding matching `Phase begin.` block; when one phase contains more than one `bar build`; when a phase has no exactly matching `Phase end.` block; or when a governed action follows `Route leak: found` before a new phase and route are established.\n\n## Route-to-command consistency (fail closed)\n\nBefore emitting the final routing envelope, check the selected route against the resolved tokens and the exact command intended for the tool call. A correct route label does not authorize a different build.\n\nFor `command_source: protocol-default`, the required base token sequence immediately after `bar build` is:\n\n- `selected: craft` → `craft`\n- `selected: inductive` → `probe depends atomic`\n- `selected: audit` → `make witness full ground presume atomic`\n\nAdmitted optional tokens may extend the base sequence; they must not replace it. In particular, `make gate falsify` and `make witness full ground presume atomic gate falsify` are not substitutes for `craft`. Do not manually expand a named workflow into a guessed equivalent. The actual command must contain the same canonical token sequence recorded in `resolved_tokens` and must exactly match the envelope’s `command` field.\n\nFor the Audit default, `ground` binds the result to stated requirements and available evidence before `presume` resolves remaining reversible choices. The resulting assumptions must retain their revision triggers. Missing evidence cannot be filled by presumption, and irreversible, production, externally consequential, or authority-bearing choices remain deferred.\n\nPreserve genuine user-directed overrides under the existing resolution rules. A general task approval such as “yes” or “let’s do it” does not by itself express a different workflow preference. Assent to an explicitly offered workflow or token combination can express that preference. Do not invent a user preference, or change `command_source` to `user-directed`, merely to excuse an assistant-selected mismatch.\n\nBefore the final envelope, emit `Route-command check: pass` only after these comparisons succeed. On mismatch, emit `Route-command check: blocked — <specific mismatch>`, correct the command or resolve a genuine user preference, and repeat the check. Do not execute the build while blocked. Keep the final envelope immediately adjacent to its exact tool call; the check belongs before the envelope, not between it and execution.\n\nIf a mismatched build has already executed, its output does not authorize the intended routed work. Stop that work, close the affected phase as blocked, and start a correctly routed phase before continuing. A later corrected label cannot retroactively validate the earlier command.\n\n## Mechanical dispatch amendment\n\nThis amendment precedes and supersedes the ordered routing predicates when its predicate matches.\n\n**0. Mechanical:** the immediate step is fully determined by explicit inputs, an already accepted procedure, and objectively checkable outputs. It requires no induction, deduction, semantic adjudication, policy choice, architecture choice, audit judgment, or acceptance decision. Examples include rebuilding accepted source, copying an accepted artifact, creating prescribed directories and modes, computing hashes, validating an exact fingerprint, running specified checks, and applying an already reviewed literal change.\n\nA mechanical step is not a governed Bar build. Skip Bar and record the fixed inputs, prescribed operation, expected outputs, and objective verification. Stop on mismatch. If execution exposes an unexpected failure, ambiguity, competing correction, causal inference, or policy choice, the step immediately ceases to be mechanical; stop and redispatch from predicate 1. Mechanical classification cannot authorize diagnosis or repair.\n\nFor delegation, prefer a registered lightweight write-capable `mechanical` worker. Use `Explore` only for read-only mechanical retrieval. If no `mechanical` worker is registered, perform the step in-thread or use the ordinary write-capable general-purpose worker without adding Bar ceremony. The worker must receive the exact inputs, operations, stop conditions, prohibited actions, and verification checks; it must not broaden scope or interpret mismatches.\n\nMechanical steps are excluded from the phase-opening, routing-envelope, Bar skill-order, and route-leak requirements below because no `bar build` occurs. A mixed task must still separate mechanical execution from any inductive, craft, or audit phase.\n\n\n### Mechanical retrieval worker correction\n\nFor read-only repository retrieval about symbols, definitions, call relationships, implementation seams, or dependency impact, prefer `lsp-trace-investigator` whenever an exact READY managed session is available. Its bounded server-reported evidence and custody limits must remain explicit. Use `Explore` only for non-semantic filesystem discovery, filename/pattern lookup, or when no functional managed LSP Trace session exists. Neither retrieval worker may perform mutation.\n\n\n### Mechanical boundary correction\n\nA small, obvious, or fully diagnosed code change is not thereby mechanical. Any step that creates or changes executable semantics, validation behavior, failure classification, security/custody behavior, schema acceptance, or a guard requires a counterfactual witness and routes to Craft, even when the intended edit is one line and the cause is known. Mechanical application is limited to byte-for-byte or instruction-for-instruction execution of an already accepted change whose semantic decision and counterfactual witness were completed earlier; the mechanical step may transport or install that accepted artifact but may not author, reinterpret, or extend it. “Straightforward” describes effort, not derivation.\n\n### Bar command-order correction\n\nThe Bar skill-order check applies before the first `bar` CLI invocation of any kind in a session, including `bar --help`, `bar skills get`, and `bar build`. A successful `bar skills list` must appear first. If any Bar command precedes it, disclose `Skill order: violated`; the violation cannot be repaired retroactively, though running `bar skills list` and loading the required skill permits subsequent builds.\n\n\n## Craft-derived mechanical execution plans\n\nA Craft phase may close the semantic decisions and emit a **Mechanical Execution Plan** whose individual units qualify for predicate 0. This separates derivation from execution: Craft owns the governing properties, alternatives, counterfactual witness, and acceptance boundary; delegated children may then perform fully prescribed transformations without repeating Craft.\n\nEach mechanical unit must bind all of the following:\n- the accepted derivation result and immutable requirements/evidence revision;\n- exact inputs and expected outputs;\n- a prescribed transformation with no unresolved alternative;\n- a disjoint write set or explicit single-writer ownership;\n- objective executable verification gates;\n- prohibited actions and semantic boundaries;\n- stop-and-escalate conditions for ambiguity, unexpected failure, overlap, or missing evidence.\n\nThe plan is mechanical only if execution requires no architecture, schema, custody, validation, failure-classification, policy, or acceptance decision. A child may not amend requirements, choose among alternatives, weaken assertions, repair an underspecified plan, or broaden the write set. Any such need invalidates predicate 0 and returns the unit to the ordered routing predicates.\n\nParallel execution is permitted only across units with disjoint ownership and independent gates. The parent retains integration and acceptance responsibility. A plan labeled mechanical without the complete bindings above has no exempting force.\n\n\n## Craft command correction\n\nThis correction supersedes every earlier bare `craft` command and every route-to-command rule that requires `bar build craft`. The canonical Craft route is now:\n\n```text\nbar build plan witness ground gate falsify atomic --subject \"<work and governing properties>\"\n```\n\nAccordingly, predicate 2 selects `craft` but resolves the protocol-default tokens to `plan witness ground gate falsify atomic`. For `command_source: protocol-default`, `selected: craft` requires that exact base token sequence immediately after `bar build`. For a user-directed Craft request, preserve the user-directed resolution rules, but an explicit request for `bar build plan witness ground gate falsify atomic` is already canonical and requires no normalization.\n\nThe route envelope must record `resolved_tokens: plan witness ground gate falsify atomic`. The `gate` and `falsify` admission substructures remain mandatory and must be `admitted`, because both tokens are part of the canonical Craft sequence. If a qualifying prior result, counterfactual property, distinguishing guard, or violating state cannot be named, Craft is blocked rather than silently dropping either token.\n\nThe plan token makes the immediate Craft output a plan artifact. It does not by itself make the plan mechanical: the Craft-derived mechanical execution-plan requirements below must still be satisfied and accepted before predicate 0 can govern delegated execution.\n\n\n## Craft command correction clarification\n\nThis clarification supersedes the preceding **Craft command correction** wherever it generalized the mechanical-handoff command to all Craft routes.\n\nThe ordinary deductive Craft route remains:\n\n```text\nbar build craft --subject \"<work and governing properties>\"\n```\n\nAccordingly, predicate 2 and the route-to-command consistency rule continue to require the base token `craft` for normal executable-behavior or guard derivation.\n\nUse the following specialized Craft sequence only when the immediate output is a Craft-derived Mechanical Execution Plan intended to close semantics before delegated predicate-0 execution:\n\n```text\nbar build plan witness ground gate falsify atomic --subject \"<mechanical handoff plan and governing properties>\"\n```\n\nFor that specialized phase, the route remains `selected: craft`, while `resolved_tokens` is `plan witness ground gate falsify atomic`; `gate` and `falsify` must be admitted with concrete prior evidence and a counterfactual guard. The resulting plan has no mechanical exempting force until parent acceptance confirms immutable inputs, exact transformations, disjoint ownership, objective gates, prohibited actions, and stop-and-escalate conditions.\n\nDo not replace ordinary `craft` with the specialized handoff sequence, and do not use bare `craft` when the immediate requested artifact is specifically a Mechanical Execution Plan for delegated execution.\n\n## Bar-result completion gate before governed mutation\n\nThis section governs every substantive Bar-built phase and supersedes any reading of “execution of the prompt produced by that build” that treats a build result, route label, or phase opening as completed derivation. Before the first Write, Edit, or Bash file mutation for that phase, require the matching `bar build` tool result at an earlier transcript position. Read its complete TOKENS, COMPOSITION RULES, and FORMAT instructions. For each listed token and composition, require its own applicable help tool result and the load/activation lines the build specifies; require its load-count confirmation and `Token loads complete.`. Require one `Token derivations:` span for that build and phase, closed by `Derived stance complete.`, with every build-required derivation line and any required completion sentinel appearing before the mutation. A truncated build/help result, missing instruction, incomplete span, or ambiguous phase attachment is not completion.\n\nImmediately before that first mutation emit `Build-to-action check:` with the phase ID, exact owning build tool-result identity, listed required tokens and compositions, their prior help-result and load-line identities, the closed derivation span, and the proposed action. Emit `Build completion: pass` only when all are present at earlier transcript positions and attached to this phase. Otherwise emit `Build completion: blocked — <missing prerequisite>`, stop the mutation, complete the missing obligation, and repeat the check. A later test, a retrospective derivation, or a copied completion claim cannot authorize the earlier edit. Repeat this check for a new build or changed phase; it is not a substitute for route selection or substantive review of the reasoning.\n\nCorrective counterexample: retain a matching `Phase begin.`, `Route:`, and `bar build` result but remove the required token loads or the closed `Token derivations:` span, then attempt the first Edit. The check must say `Build completion: blocked`. If it says pass, treat the gate as ineffective, revise it, and repeat this counterexample before mutation. A check that blocks terminates this counterexample; complete the missing obligations before retrying the action.\n\n## Links\n\n- [[20260717225137-3213|Bar tokens shape a response as jointly applied constraints]] [grounded-by] {draft} — The code-change Bar protocol is grounded by the model of tokens as jointly applied constraints.\n\n## Backlinks (9)\n\n- [[20260708175824-9205|Friction: incomplete bar token compliance can force costly restart work]] [questions] {draft} — this friction challenges whether the current craft protocol is operationally sticky\n- [[20260714165746-6090|Observation: Luna 5.6 followed derivation protocols less reliably]] [questions] {draft} — This observation questions whether all selected models can reliably execute the craft derivation protocol.\n- [[20260715161218-3359|Friction: brittle bar derivation sequencing is easy to interrupt]] [questions] {draft} — This friction challenges whether the craft protocol remains usable when composed with other mandatory workflow protocols.\n- [[20260716195542-7313|Friction: protocol checkpoints can become accidental turn boundaries]] [questions] {draft} — this friction challenges whether craft gates reliably preserve autonomous continuation\n- [[20260717225137-3213|Bar tokens shape a response as jointly applied constraints]] [refines] {reviewed} — explains how bar tokens jointly constrain a response and how to audit their phrase-level effects\n- [[20260823201547-8354|Recent Craft evidence suggests historical continuation friction is mitigated but not resolved]] [supports] {reviewed} — One compliant current-session Craft execution provides bounded evidence that the present pre-edit Craft protocol can operate without reproducing the July continuation failures.\n- [[20260904024849-8920|Protocol: use Bar probe depends atomic for inductive work]] [extends] {reviewed} — Adds the complementary observation-first derivation mode to the property-first Craft protocol.\n- [[20260907184417-9665|Protocol: re-derive Bar-governed work in the executing subagent thread]] [extends] — Makes derivation ownership explicit across delegation, with narrow predeclared exceptions and parent acceptance checks.\n- [[20260930034926-2429|Protocol: read complete unmodified Bar output before relying on it]] [refines] {draft} — Requires complete Bar-output evidence when applying the routing and build-result gates\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791906019433},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:40:19.434Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"toolResult","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_Ksg14Tc0lgTqapiahvyG9qKb|fc_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e2a32c8191a7b00e3ca7805ba9","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nid: 20260904024849-8920\ntitle: 'Protocol: use Bar probe depends atomic for inductive work'\ntype: protocol\nstatus: reviewed\ntags: []\napplies_when: before substantive work whose artifact or understanding must be generalized from bounded interventions, observed resistance, or discovered dependencies; re-evaluate when the derivation direction changes\nexpires: 2026-10-04T00:00:00Z\ncreated: 2026-09-04T02:48:49.846166Z\nmodified: 2026-10-01T11:22:43.700383-07:00\n---\nfreshness: aging (11 days ago — may need recheck)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n## Protocol\n\nBefore substantive inductive work, run `bar build probe depends atomic --subject \"<question, bounded intervention, and observation target>\"` and use the result to expand understanding through bounded probes, explicit dependency discovery, and independently observable file-modifying steps.\n\nClassify by artifact-derivation direction rather than artifact type or feedback-loop appearance. Use this protocol when observations come first and the resulting map, rule, or structure is generalized from them. Do not invoke it merely because work is exploratory in tone. If stated properties or premises already govern the artifact and the task derives what must follow, use the complementary Craft protocol.\n\n`atomic` governs file-modifying calls specifically: commit the scope and symbol before each call, make one independently observable change, and obtain a clean run result afterward. Reclassify whenever the work changes derivation direction.\n\nIf command grammar or token semantics change, consult the current Bar skill and CLI rather than guessing. The current Bar skill requires `bar skills list` before the first `bar skills get` or `bar build` in a session.\n\nThe 2026-10-01 Pi foreground fallback probe showed that a child opening assignment must be compared with the Agent invocation prompt, not its short description.\n\n## Links\n\n- [[20260707222228-6219|Protocol: route requirement-governed work by derivation step]] [extends] {reviewed} — Adds the complementary observation-first derivation mode to the property-first Craft protocol.\n- [[20260717225137-3213|Bar tokens shape a response as jointly applied constraints]] [grounded-by] {reviewed} — The inductive workflow relies on Bar tokens acting jointly as task and method constraints.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791906019434},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:40:19.434Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"**Planning mandatory Bar skills list**","thinkingSignature":"{\"type\":\"reasoning\",\"id\":\"rs_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e6d1f0819190831a7ada7882a4\",\"summary\":[{\"type\":\"summary_text\",\"text\":\"**Planning mandatory Bar skills list**\"}],\"content\":[],\"encrypted_content\":\"gAAAAABqzlDovcKozqwxKNJ6uZ7BTijXq6fmG5JDrJWumPtUXD99bk-fR1saJ9PmUKxDBvvQzqVei2HRDsuhPAqm3t_AEVoN8hOjQS7378gs1__DgmlzkYUqAC14QRnh2gHu3veU-S02BxOUXFxl95DwXNJuYGUXDwT1CIMieA39-HKV6sOEsqDgMSrpFD1RPFB3UEJ7WJdwORL8KCqWLMvCt6JpnIwJtRxGtIRGpSEETLlP4QcgcfTmmosdp_nOgPpImewyiWPwZioy8maiDqaOz8qZpC2Zp9Ivj5Ad7AfhAlUOAUeozw7BuRkuT7m2daxBhgrlzC8PgtMRRzZvqPU_fiAokgNUJh6tNtapCIz5zv0J10CjcZs8TKcvMgI9PKDDU0TsNQGZkMuzBY3Cv6nlZ1XZ9TVJVG30QqrweV6FCOjAp1CCO6eeKYD8S62Cd9towUv6pzY1evfWZl3jMzFfMonYuuLwOq48WuCK2GR7z6zedVEi7LouHueYELLMhDFQfsTAgSL00-7e568dd1G1mxfHUDEuTrlQ8vDHjby1qEz2dj_ryhq38muQWvhn2fPfCcWIEHYVuGhllqbncJhEGQSF5U1UZju0qTY_NWiDTzdNco8XJHL6th1PtXI03dbZyRvcaT5NKLE7B4Nyge7dePdpa4hzRJu2Fvjp8OmZWwxbIypCZMw2e9w8Hta23R86ir8HR67ndCnaQrb5a18M4UZRyyQEOYa2djDQ55Vq_j6p8dGe0w_6Phy5qyUFPcOvfmiZLa78tgu3FHFy9pU-wRGhUhdaqzj3MSPBuZRLANfAc0xQM4LI5QwVKlK9_lP6dtQZP0Jt3thAu6LVu-6Cjcac3abAtHEVLsRv17XFJR59Ay6L9HH_q9elZCgWGYLW7FYIxyAGz_dfX7v-4dWbur7P7_S7BZlXqD0yR8P1Z6WptdhE6-wc5Ay7PbJxilsJjTk0WKb31nl7xdaonNikBy_rplp4iK-gmvQRpv_GO5TdnwevIyhc_RFuXUsUWn5vxbJBE3y796KE_snv8wVAoGK2QI7-_JSUQ-aR93hddWQtKc44Iwr9tK7Hj7z1bzmDqT4cnT4qccwrAVBgiR0tChUONURUVXXnjecBNIzqQ9enIMrUCRc6rvEwxOeQ0ul3h9eXU5NB8pnvPRQDXvwSU7QCgAtibWnTRambzzxslao0o_GsDuS78v5SZeTDag7zaMkaKH0U_2_j27sKDqbyQjtRXkl2PG8QxmjjHvqeMnZDZ15ICbwndkQ8pcinMbTA6b-lnisGwR8CSPGxUy3caMvU7NTExq61maChJSjletWgJG4WvATikl8-0x_gwEBDcUCJLRtU9KI3EEkhCUsu9QMiwNM7QI6Fw-pBmnFEwUWdq24aZYZlUUs5bM88qavwII1er62zDivhPYUW05GcGWYAf7jPlBBdUVsQ3LCMBkNn24_N1zDVkgoALmCPXi1Wy-pADt7q4eLkUVoDuzSo5EfRJkxagRG08tJglLE6GYA8-4vmEil6hV9XLang7nSSKhmHefs-MwPAiIdRDCAuLed52dy_Lwqqb7tDGn2vS1q5v5WNoLzcXi5sDCLZ3XSZXE25voBSxw5aTUA9QLMZv2_7hVzvWYbk64pR99tPSMf2wwHpAt6sU6ak-27iW8C8F3ewPf7RpbISV7SAqPz0b3C693dkbX7d3rmvVQ3KIJL_8Xec7lUZx3oi98s3cBCJ76watTb1K6oIzGjdqfc50E1iPz8CxYkOx1t1umoF-_bWbJnCX0Z9RXqMibZUkSWYTiB62TmlN7uasYbfzgzJCJAy1r9koH3-kvAlRJixZsAlLvOS2PLgXyrEMeqiTzRFoXYMYl4nwvZ9KnVeKEDX667y43u05JFW24bHkyYeblxIYyeH0aXONNpVOe48LqW1NqeOUX2G_0hGZYJvmyfDkFKM_dOPbQ3-Lg-COoMDj_I-thWMUzybDZiYuHJLIQv0xrfKLsAufu6ymO6Kovpv7BMLvlE4yEpZWQR1mPtWhKeLvHrlQ-OYY5gf37RC-421rqr3DUBb\"}"},{"type":"text","text":"The routing protocol applies: the immediate artifact is an independent review derived from stated acceptance contracts and available evidence, so this is an Audit phase rather than a mechanical-only checksum run. The inductive protocol does not own the phase because observations do not define the acceptance criteria. The related feature-inventory notes are not needed because this request concerns one explicit acceptance package rather than product feature identity.\n\nSkill order: pass","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e7a5f08191bf8414ade1e7448b\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_03ea40db11c64ef29d67bfc0e5c4a694|fc_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e9db448191883273efb6945ad4","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"bar skills list","timeout":30}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":45371,"output":155,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":42,"totalTokens":45526,"cost":{"input":0.18148399999999998,"output":0.0031000000000000003,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.18458399999999997}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791906019436,"responseId":"resp_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e49c44819185ed1cc415919836","rawStopReason":"completed","thinkingLevel":"minimal"},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:41:00.073Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"toolResult","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_03ea40db11c64ef29d67bfc0e5c4a694|fc_086e788cb8bf6932016ace50e9db448191883273efb6945ad4","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"  bar-autopilot        Use when user wants bar to automatically shape every response — detects and...\n  bar-dictionary       Use when user asks which token fits an intent — looks up bar tokens by inte...\n  bar-manual           Use when user wants to learn bar commands or build prompts manually — guide...\n  bar-steer            Use when the user wants to drive through a space of bar options step-by-step ...\n  bar-suggest          Use when user wants help choosing an approach or isn't sure where to start �...\n  bar-workflow         Use when user wants to chain multiple steps or build a sequence — construct...\n  For named sequences (e.g. token-rewrite): load bar-workflow, then run bar sequence show <name>\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791906060073},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:41:00.074Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_IMc7gO7cruYoCVzWswACoQ0b|fc_086e788cb8bf6932016ace51101b6881919b7ace42f4cb1fc9","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"bar skills get bar-manual","timeout":30}}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","usage":{"input":7417,"output":27,"cacheRead":38272,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":45716,"cost":{"input":0.029668,"output":0.00054,"cacheRead":0.015308800000000001,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.045516799999999996}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791906060077,"responseId":"resp_086e788cb8bf6932016ace510ca9ec81919626471988cc88f0","rawStopReason":"completed","thinkingLevel":"minimal"},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:41:04.789Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
{"isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a856f367-42c9-454","type":"toolResult","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_IMc7gO7cruYoCVzWswACoQ0b|fc_086e788cb8bf6932016ace51101b6881919b7ace42f4cb1fc9","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: bar-manual\ndescription: Use when user wants to learn bar commands or build prompts manually — guides bar CLI usage step by step.\n---\n\n# Bar Manual Usage Skill\n\n## Purpose and Preconditions\n\nThis skill helps users **manually build bar commands** when they ask how to use the bar CLI.\nUse this skill when the user wants to learn or get help with bar, not when automatically\nstructuring responses (use bar-autopilot, bar-workflow, or bar-suggest for automatic usage).\n\nThis skill **does not encode grammar**. Instead, it **teaches users to discover tokens and patterns via `bar help llm`** (or `bar help tokens` for older versions).\n\nAssumes:\n- `bar` CLI is installed and accessible\n- The user can run `bar help` commands\n- Optional: custom grammar JSON via `--grammar` or `BAR_GRAMMAR_PATH`\n\n## When to Use This Skill\n\nUse bar-manual when:\n- User asks \"How do I use bar?\"\n- User wants to learn bar command syntax\n- User asks for help building a specific bar command\n- User requests bar examples or tutorials\n\nDo NOT use bar-manual for:\n- Automatically structuring your own responses (use bar-autopilot instead)\n- Multi-step workflows (use bar-workflow instead)\n- Presenting options to users (use bar-suggest instead)\n\n## High-level Workflow\n\n### With `bar help llm` (preferred)\n\n1. **Load navigation guide**: Run `bar help llm` (no args) as a standalone Bash command — do not pipe its output. A compliant invocation produces a tool-result block containing `## Context window`. Teach users the same pattern.\n2. **Guide to relevant sections on demand**:\n   - `bar help llm --section heuristics` → Token Selection Heuristics (Choosing Method/Scope/Form)\n   - `bar help llm --section tokens` → full Token Catalog (compliant when tool-result contains `### Directional (0-1 token)`)\n   - `bar help llm --section patterns` → Usage Patterns by Task Type\n   - `bar help llm --section rules` → Composition Rules\n   - `bar help token <slug>` → deep detail for one token (definition, heuristics, distinctions)\n3. **Build recipe together**: Use discovered tokens to construct bar command\n4. **Optional exploration**: Teach `bar shuffle` for alternatives\n\n### Fallback (legacy `bar help tokens`)\n\n1. **Discover tokens**: Run `bar help tokens` or sectioned variants\n2. **Build recipe**: Guide user through token selection\n3. **Iterate**: Adjust based on needs\n4. **Optional shuffle**: Explore with `bar shuffle`\n\n## Teaching Approach\n\n### Step 1: Show the Reference\n\n**For bar versions with `bar help llm`:**\n\nTeach the user to access comprehensive reference:\n```bash\nbar help llm\n```\n\nExplain what they'll see:\n- ~500 lines of markdown documentation\n- All available tokens across 7 axes\n- Usage patterns for common scenarios\n- Token selection heuristics\n- Composition rules and constraints\n- Complete examples\n\n**For older bar versions:**\n\nFall back to discovery commands:\n```bash\nbar help tokens              # All tokens\nbar help tokens task       # Tasks only\nbar help tokens scope method # Multiple axes\nbar help tokens persona      # Persona system\n```\n\n### Step 2: Guide to Relevant Sections\n\nBased on user's request, point them to specific sections in `bar help llm`:\n\n**For \"How do I make decisions?\"** → Reference § \"Usage Patterns by Task Type\" § \"Decision-Making\"\n\n**For \"What tokens are available?\"** → Reference § \"Token Catalog\" sections\n\n**For \"How do I choose scope/method/form?\"** → Reference § \"Token Selection Heuristics\"\n\n**For \"What are the rules?\"** → Reference § \"Composition Rules\"\n\n**For \"Show me examples\"** → Reference § \"Usage Patterns by Task Type\" (8 examples)\n\n**For \"How does persona work?\"** → Reference § \"Persona System\"\n\n### Step 3: Build Command Together\n\nOnce user understands the reference:\n\n```bash\n# Token order from reference § \"Grammar Architecture\":\n# [persona] [static] [completeness] [scope...] [method...] [form] [channel] [directional]\n\n# Build command with discovered tokens\nbar build <tokens-from-reference> --subject \"user's text\"\n\n# Examples (tokens discovered from reference, not hardcoded):\n# - For decisions: reference shows patterns using decision-oriented tokens\n# - For understanding: reference shows patterns using understanding-oriented tokens\n# - For diagnosis: reference shows patterns using diagnostic-oriented tokens\n```\n\n### Step 4: Iterate and Refine\n\nGuide user through adjustments:\n- Check output quality\n- Adjust tokens based on results\n- Reference § \"Token Catalog\" for alternatives\n- Use skip sentinels (`//next`, `//:stage`) if needed\n\n### Step 5: Optional Exploration\n\nTeach shuffle for exploration:\n```bash\nbar shuffle                           # Random exploration\nbar shuffle --seed 42                 # Reproducible\nbar shuffle --include scope,method    # Focus axes\nbar shuffle --exclude persona         # Exclude axes\nbar shuffle --fill 0.8                # Adjust density\nbar shuffle --json                    # JSON output to inspect full token set\n```\n\n**Note on compound directionals:** `bar help tokens directional` lists only primitive\ndirectional tokens. Compound tokens (e.g., `fly rog`, `fip rog`, `dip ong`, `dip bog`)\nalso exist and are listed in `bar help llm` § Token Catalog § Directional. Run\n`bar shuffle --json` and inspect the `directional` field to discover compound forms in use.\n\n### Step 6: Interactive Grammar Learning (Optional)\n\nFor users who prefer to learn through interaction, point them to `bar tui2` — the\nstage-based grammar editor that teaches token selection through direct interaction:\n\n```bash\nbar tui2                        # Launch with live preview\nbar tui2 make full              # Pre-seed tokens at launch\nbar tui2 --command \"pbcopy\"     # Pre-fill the Run Command field\n```\n\nInside `bar tui2`, users progress through grammar stages (task → completeness → scope →\nmethod → form → …) with live preview of the generated prompt. The equivalent `bar build`\ncommand is shown and copyable at any time, closing the loop back to the CLI.\n\n## Command Patterns\n\n### Accessing Help\n\n**Preferred (with bar help llm):**\n```bash\n# Get comprehensive reference — no arguments, not piped\nbar help llm\n\n# Also accessible as:\nbar help reference\n\n# Per-token disambiguation (near-neighbor guidance, combination entries):\nbar guide <token>    # e.g. bar guide probe — when to use probe vs check vs fix\n```\n\n**Legacy (older bar versions):**\n```bash\n# Discover all tokens\nbar help tokens\n\n# Discover specific sections\nbar help tokens task\nbar help tokens axes\nbar help tokens scope method\nbar help tokens persona persona-intents\n```\n\n### Building Commands\n\n```bash\n# Discover tokens first (via bar help llm or bar help tokens)\n\n# Build with shorthand (order matters)\nbar build <static> <completeness> <scope> <method> <form> --subject \"text\"\n\n# Build with persona\nbar build persona=<preset> <static> <completeness> --subject \"text\"\n\n# Build with key=value overrides (all after first override must be key=value)\nbar build <static> completeness=<value> scope=<value> --subject \"text\"\n\n# Skip stages\nbar build //next <static> <completeness>\nbar build //:static <completeness> <scope>\n```\n\n### Output Handling\n\n```bash\n# Get JSON for automation\nbar build <tokens> --subject \"text\" --json\n\n# Save to file\nbar build <tokens> --subject \"text\" --output recipe.txt\n\n# Read from file\nbar build <tokens> --input prompt.txt\n\n# Use STDIN\necho \"text\" | bar build <tokens>\n```\n\n### Interactive Grammar Exploration\n\n```bash\n# Launch the stage-based grammar editor (recommended for new users)\nbar tui2\n\n# Pre-seed tokens at launch\nbar tui2 make full\n\n# Pre-fill the Run Command field (e.g. for clipboard copy workflow)\nbar tui2 --command \"pbcopy\"\n\n# Quickly scan all available token slugs with labels (ADR-0109)\nbar help tokens --plain             # category:slug<TAB>label — tab-separated when labels exist\nbar help tokens scope --plain       # scope axis only\nbar help tokens --plain | grep '^task:'  # just task lines (label included if present)\nbar help tokens --plain | cut -f1        # slugs only, strip labels\n```\n\n### Saving Presets\n\n```bash\n# After building a good command\nbar preset save my-decision-pattern\n\n# Reuse with new content\nbar preset use my-decision-pattern --subject \"new text\"\n\n# List saved presets\nbar preset list\n\n# Show preset details\nbar preset show my-decision-pattern\n```\n\n## Skill Behavior Rules\n\n- **Never invent tokens.** Always teach users to discover via `bar help llm` (preferred) or `bar help tokens` (fallback).\n- **Reference sections, don't embed content.** Point users to § sections in the reference output.\n- **Teach discovery, not memorization.** Focus on showing users how to find what they need.\n- **Validate ordering.** Token order from reference: persona → static → completeness → scope (1-2) → method (1-3) → form → channel → directional\n- **Support overrides.** After first `key=value`, all remaining tokens must be `key=value`.\n- **Use kebab-case for multi-word tokens.** Convert spaces to hyphens (e.g., \"as-kent-beck\").\n- **Offer shuffling after baseline.** A shuffle suggestion is permitted only when a prior bar build command result appears above it in the transcript — a shuffle suggestion that appears without a prior bar build result in the transcript does not satisfy this requirement.\n- **\"Run the command\" means execute + show output.** Execute the bar command and show results.\n\n## Recommended Conversation Flow\n\n**User:** \"Help me build a prompt for [topic].\"\n\n**Assistant (with bar help llm):**\n1. \"Let me show you the comprehensive reference: `bar help llm`\"\n2. \"For your use case, look at the § 'Usage Patterns by Task Type' section\"\n3. \"Based on the reference, we can use tokens from § 'Token Catalog'\"\n4. \"Let's build: `bar build <tokens-from-reference> --subject '<topic>'`\"\n5. \"Want alternatives? We can use `bar shuffle` to explore\"\n\n**Assistant (legacy fallback):**\n1. \"Let's discover valid tokens: `bar help tokens`\"\n2. \"Based on the output, which tokens fit your needs?\"\n3. \"We'll build: `bar build <chosen-tokens> --subject '<topic>'`\"\n4. \"Want to shuffle for alternatives?\"\n\n## Example Teaching Session\n\n**With bar help llm:**\n```bash\n# Step 1: Show comprehensive reference\nbar help llm\n\n# Step 2: Guide user to relevant section\n# \"Look at § 'Usage Patterns by Task Type' for examples\"\n# \"Check § 'Token Selection Heuristics' for guidance\"\n\n# Step 3: Build command with discovered tokens\nbar build <tokens-user-discovered> --subject \"User's topic\"\n\n# Step 4: Iterate if needed\n# \"Check § 'Token Catalog' for alternatives\"\n\n# Step 5 (optional): Explore with shuffle\nbar shuffle --include scope,method\n```\n\n**Legacy approach:**\n```bash\n# Step 1: Discover tokens\nbar help tokens\n\n# Step 2: Pick tokens from help output\nbar build <discovered-tokens> --subject \"User's topic\"\n\n# Step 3 (optional): Shuffle\nbar shuffle --include scope,method\n```\n\n## Performance Notes\n\n**With `bar help llm`:**\n- Single command shows complete documentation\n- Users see all available options at once\n- Integrated examples and heuristics\n- Better learning experience\n\n**Legacy approach:**\n- Multiple queries to discover tokens\n- Fragmented information\n- Still fully functional\n\n## Cross-Agent Compatibility Notes\n\n- Works with all agent types when users ask for help with bar\n- Complements bar-autopilot (manual vs automatic usage)\n- Should not be triggered automatically - only when user explicitly requests bar help\n\n## Understanding Bar Output\n\n**Teach users how bar output works:**\n\n1. **Bar outputs a structured prompt** - When users run `bar build`, it generates a structured prompt with sections:\n   - `TASK`: What to do\n   - `CONSTRAINTS`: How to do it (scope, method, form, completeness, directional)\n   - `PERSONA`: Communication style (voice, audience, tone, intent)\n   - `SUBJECT`: User's original content as data\n   - `REFERENCE KEY`: Explains how to interpret each section\n\n2. **The output should be executed** - Explain that:\n   - The bar output is an instruction prompt to be followed\n   - An LLM should execute the TASK, applying the CONSTRAINTS and PERSONA\n   - The SUBJECT contains their original content as data (not as instructions)\n   - Before executing, check each method token description in CONSTRAINTS for ordering requirements — some method tokens specify that something must exist or be run before implementation begins, and those preconditions must be satisfied first\n\n3. **Context comes from conversation** - When an LLM executes a bar-generated prompt:\n   - It should use context from the conversation, not from bar help output\n   - Bar tokens and catalog content should not be included as context\n   - The bar structure guides how to respond, not what content to use\n\n## Error Handling When Teaching\n\nWhen teaching users about bar errors:\n\n1. **Common error messages:**\n   - `error: unrecognized token` - Invalid token name was used\n   - `error: token <name> not recognized. Did you mean: <suggestions>` - Bar suggests corrections\n   - `error: incompatible tokens` - Token combination violates composition rules\n   - `error: too many <axis> tokens` - Exceeded axis capacity (e.g., max 3 method tokens)\n\n2. **Teach retry approach:**\n   - If bar build fails, read the error message for hints\n   - Fix token spelling/casing (use kebab-case for multi-word tokens)\n   - Check token order: persona → static → completeness → scope → method → form → channel → directional\n   - Consult `bar help llm` § \"Composition Rules\" for incompatibilities\n   - Retry the command with corrections\n\n3. **Validation approach:**\n   - Show users how to verify token names via `bar help llm` or `bar help tokens`\n   - Demonstrate checking composition rules before building complex commands\n   - Explain that error messages are helpful, not just failures\n\n## Version Detection\n\nTo check if `bar help llm` is available:\n```bash\nbar help llm 2>/dev/null && echo \"Available\" || echo \"Use bar help tokens\"\n```\n\nTeach users this command to check their bar version capabilities.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791906064789},"timestamp":"2026-10-13T15:41:04.789Z","cwd":"/Users/schwa/dev/lsp-trace"}
